<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Karaman</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Larende</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Karaman is a city in south-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, known in Byzantine and Seljuk times as Larende. From the late 13th century it was a main seat of the Karamanid dynasty, which gave the city its modern name and built mosques and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a> there. After the Ottoman conquest in the 15th century it gave its name to the Ottoman province of Karaman, and it is now a provincial capital.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karaman.jpg" alt="Karaman" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dosseman · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karaman_castle_4868.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Karaman lies in Central Anatolia, north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taurus-mountains">Taurus Mountains</a> and about 100 km south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a>, at an average elevation of 1,039 metres. It has a cold semi-arid climate with hot, dry summers, cold, snowy winters and almost 3,000 hours of sunshine a year. The city takes its name from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karaman-bey">Karaman Bey</a>, one of the rulers of the Karamanid dynasty. Its earlier name, Laranda, comes from the Luwian Larawanda, meaning a sandy place, and in Hittite times it was known as Lānda.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Little is known of the early history of Laranda. It is first named in the writings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a>, who says that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perdiccas">Perdiccas</a> and King Philip, after defeating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariarathes-i-of-cappadocia">Ariarathes I of Cappadocia</a>, set out to punish Laranda and Isauria for killing Balacrus, the general whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> had appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a> of the region. Laranda was destroyed, later became a seat of Isaurian pirates and for a time was controlled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antipater">Antipater</a> of Derbe, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amyntas-of-galatia">Amyntas of Galatia</a> killed him. In the early third century CE it was the seat of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, Neon, who approved the preaching of the layman Euelpis there. It belonged to the Roman and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> captured it in the early 12th century.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> occupied the city in 1190 and the Armenian Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> held it from 1211 to 1216. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karaman-bey">Karaman Bey</a> took it in 1256 and gave it his name, and from 1275 it was the capital of the Karamanid beylik. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> preacher Bahaeddin Veled arrived in 1222 with his family, and the emir had a madrasah built for them. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rumi">Rumi</a> married his wife Gevher Hatun in Karaman, and Rumi's mother died there in 1224. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> conquered the Karamanids in 1468 and moved the capital of the province to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a> in 1483.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Karaman is the seat of Karaman Province and had 175,390 inhabitants in 2022. From the Karamanid period it keeps the ruins of a castle and some walls, two mosques and a madrasah. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aktekke-mosque">Aktekke Mosque</a>, also called the Mader-i Mevlana Cami, built in 1370 to replace the original madrasah, holds the graves of Rumi's mother and other family members. The poet Yunus Emre, who lived about 1238 to 1320, spent his later years in Karaman and is believed to lie buried beside the Yunus Emre Mosque.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Karaman” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 1250 they rose against the weakened Sultanate of Rum, and <b>Karaman's</b> son Mehmed Bey briefly occupied Konya in 1277, where he reportedly decreed that Turkish alone be used in government.</blockquote><a href="/state/karamanids">Karamanids</a></li></ul></section>
